{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1322 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "1bbc850a06f904e96f2a4aecd391db30ec39d017e2af9b5de8fb57fc8d11414d" :edb/db-before-t 1321 :edb/db-after-t 1322 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534634 50 #inst "2026-10-08T03:01:54.831Z" 13194139534634 true] [17592186184029 1100 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x=2$ is given by\n\n$f(x)=1 + 4(x-2) - 12(x-2)^2$.\n\nWhat is the value of $f''(2)$?" 13194139534634 false] [17592186184029 1100 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x=2$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)=1 + 4(x-2) - 12(x-2)^2.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $f''(2)?$" 13194139534634 true] [17592186184029 1145 "This is an expansion about the point $x=2$, so the coefficient of the second-degree term is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2 f''(2)\n$$\n\nIn the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the second-degree term is $-12$, so we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2 f''(2) = -12\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad f''(2) = -24\n$$" 13194139534634 false] [17592186184029 1145 "This is an expansion about the point $x=2,$ so the coefficient of the second-degree term is $\\dfrac 1 2 f''(2).$ In the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the second-degree term is $-12,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2 f''(2) = -12\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad f''(2) = -24.\n$$" 13194139534634 true] [17592186184030 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/assets/8b630e44d49440844d668f395dd5d85676e1824f283edb86194a88a62c78b3f7.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y = f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-1?$\n\n- $-1+2(x+1)-3(x+1)^2$\n\n- $-1+4(x+1)+(x+1)^2$\n\n- $-1-5(x+1)-2(x+1)^2$" 13194139534634 true] [17592186184030 1100 "The figure above shows the graph of a function $y = f(x)$. Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-1$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3825/Source/Images/e67c9dfab10704ddb8dfa9e9559f5e0d.png)\n\n1. $-1+2(x+1)-3(x+1)^2$\n2. $-1+4(x+1)+(x+1)^2$\n3. $-1-5(x+1)-2(x+1)^2$" 13194139534634 false] [17592186184030 1145 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\nP_2(x)=f(-1)+f'(-1)(x+1)+\\dfrac{f''(-1)}{2}(x+1)^2\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(-1) <0$. Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n- $f'(-1) >0$, because the slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is positive. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n- $f''(-1) <0$, because the graph is concave down at $x=-1$. Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\nOnly option I meets all of the above criteria." 13194139534634 false] [17592186184030 1145 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-1$ is \n\n$$\nP_2(x)=f(-1)+f'(-1)(x+1)+\\dfrac{f''(-1)}{2}(x+1)^2.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(-1)  <0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\n- $f'(-1) >0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is positive. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- $f''(-1) <0,$ because the graph is concave down at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\nOnly option I meets all of the above criteria." 13194139534634 true] [17592186184128 1020 #edb/ref 17592186299996 13194139534634 true] [17592186184128 1020 #edb/ref 17592186299999 13194139534634 true] [17592186184128 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300002 13194139534634 true] [17592186184128 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300006 13194139534634 true] [17592186184128 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300013 13194139534634 true] [17592186184129 1020 #edb/ref 17592186299989 13194139534634 true] [17592186184129 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300020 13194139534634 true] [17592186184129 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300027 13194139534634 true] [17592186184472 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534634 true] [17592186184472 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/assets/ef5618f26d499eeffd693d1f0dc674761bcfd5762b9620044b295870983799a0.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $f.$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=-3?$" 13194139534634 true] [17592186184472 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3825/Source/Images/q-15523.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $f$. Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x =-3$?" 13194139534634 false] [17592186184472 1145 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-3$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(-3)+{f}^{′}(-3)(x+3)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(-3)}{2}(x+3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(-3)<0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\n- ${f}^{′}(-3)>0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=-3$ is positive. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-3)<0,$ because the graph is concave down at $x=-3.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=-2+(x+3)-(x+3)^{2}.\n$$" 13194139534634 true] [17592186294399 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035241/assets/e483403ebe85920111334dacfc22dc907ab3c33cd38b33361d7634132032c811.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=1?$" 13194139534634 false] [17592186294399 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/assets/e483403ebe85920111334dacfc22dc907ab3c33cd38b33361d7634132032c811.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=1?$" 13194139534634 true] [17592186299989 1017 #edb/ref 17592186299990 13194139534634 true] [17592186299989 1089 "q-125822" 13194139534634 true] [17592186299989 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534634 true] [17592186299989 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/assets/53b9bfe6c2b3109a14fa42651fe402fc546f6dceb1526459500fbdf537565976.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=-2?$" 13194139534634 true] [17592186299989 1113 #uuid "4a6c97dc-b222-56a6-abb0-4345542b7676" 13194139534634 true] [17592186299989 1145 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-2$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(-2)+{f}^{′}(-2)(x+2)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(-2)}{2}(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(-2)>0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{′}(-2)<0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=-2$ is negative. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-2)>0,$ because the graph is concave up at $x=-2.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=1-3(x+2)+5(x+2)^{2}.\n$$" 13194139534634 true] [17592186299990 1000 #edb/ref 17592186299991 13194139534634 true] [17592186299990 1000 #edb/ref 17592186299992 13194139534634 true] [17592186299990 1000 #edb/ref 17592186299993 13194139534634 true] [17592186299990 1000 #edb/ref 17592186299994 13194139534634 true] [17592186299990 1000 #edb/ref 17592186299995 13194139534634 true] [17592186299990 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534634 true] [17592186299990 1041 "selection" 13194139534634 true] [17592186299990 1104 #edb/ref 17592186299994 13194139534634 true] [1759218