e] [17592186300019 1034 "-1" 13194139534634 true] [17592186300019 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534634 true] [17592186300019 1111 #uuid "d55db6dc-ccdc-58f4-8b4d-3d195a74927b" 13194139534634 true] [17592186300020 1017 #edb/ref 17592186300021 13194139534634 true] [17592186300020 1089 "q-83728" 13194139534634 true] [17592186300020 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534634 true] [17592186300020 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/assets/bb52a7975c917c7fe310eb29ae1daaa3438a906b84771ae5ce6998c055c4e6f7.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=3?$" 13194139534634 true] [17592186300020 1113 #uuid "a26b2381-0bbb-5cae-8272-9296c98fffe6" 13194139534634 true] [17592186300020 1145 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=3$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(3)+{f}^{′}(3)(x-3)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(3)}{2}(x-3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(3)>0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{′}(3)>0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=3$ is positive. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{″}(3)<0,$ because the graph is concave down at $x=3.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=3+2(x-3)-(x-3)^{2}.\n$$" 13194139534634 true] [17592186300021 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300022 13194139534634 true] [17592186300021 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300023 13194139534634 true] [17592186300021 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300024 13194139534634 true] [17592186300021 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300025 13194139534634 true] [17592186300021 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300026 13194139534634 true] [17592186300021 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534634 true] [17592186300021 1041 "selection" 13194139534634 true] [17592186